{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#128","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#128","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#128\",\"parent_revision\":\"b54779e13d28975209b0b7a56aa952eff2e72c042428af79b5aa9b77b1fd66db\",\"root_revision\":\"b54779e13d28975209b0b7a56aa952eff2e72c042428af79b5aa9b77b1fd66db\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEight tests in `packages/skilllint/tests/test_rule_registry.py` call `RULE_REGISTRY.clear()` on the module-global registry with no fixture restoring it. The global is left in whatever state the last test wrote.\\n\\nAny later test that reads `RULE_REGISTRY` therefore passes or fails depending on execution order. This surfaced while drafting the `test_docs_url_parity` assertions during #103: an assertion walking `RULE_REGISTRY` passed standalone and failed under `pytest-randomly` depending on ordering.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current Status\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nNothing is failing today. The #103 assertion was written to avoid depending on the ordering rather than to rely on it, so no shipped test is affected. The defect is latent and will bite the next test that reads the registry.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAn autouse fixture snapshotting and restoring the dict, or `monkeypatch.setattr` per test.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Also Worth A One Word Change\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`test_rule_registry.py:181` uses `\\\"FM008\\\"` as an arbitrary sample rule id (`RULE_REGISTRY[\\\"FM008\\\"] = _entry(\\\"FM008\\\", ...)`). Harmless \u2014 a local fixture, not a registration \u2014 but it makes grepping for genuine FM008 usage noisier now that the rule is being retired. Renaming to an obviously-synthetic id such as `\\\"ZZ001\\\"` keeps that grep clean.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- #103 \u2014 where this was found\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"7228d86544af708ef8b216d86efd615d753091a584e7b91842e3a0e7665da327\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDORXxKvc8AAAABXP-IFA\"}"}